        =====================================================================================
        🔴 WHY THIS IS ADDITIVE, AND WHY Selected_Broker__c (Text 255) SURVIVES.
        =====================================================================================
        Selected_Broker__c is NOT converted in place and is NOT retired. It cannot be, because
        the ON-MARKET BOV chain writes a String into it and that chain is out of scope:

            BovSubmissionTriggerHandler.cls:150   builds the map of submission to firm
            BovSubmissionTriggerHandler.cls:163   stamps Selected_Broker__c from
                                                  BOV_Submission__c.Broker_Firm__c, a Text(255)
            BovSubmissionService.cls:180          stamps the same String on a broker replacement

        Converting the Text field to Lookup(Contact) would leave both of those writers assigning
        a String to an Id field. That is a compile failure in Apex, and if it somehow reached the
        org a Broker_Firm__c value such as 'CBRE' is not an Id, so the on-market broker
        appointment would fail outright. Those writers cannot be changed to compensate, because
        the on-market path is explicitly out of scope this wave.

        SO THE TWO FIELDS NOW HAVE DIFFERENT JOBS:
            Broker__c            the DECISION. Off-market only. The gate in
                                 DispositionApprovalService reads THIS field, not the text.
            Selected_Broker__c   the DENORMALISED DISPLAY VALUE. Off-market it is STAMPED FROM
                                 THIS LOOKUP by DispositionBrokerStampService in before-context.
                                 On-market it keeps its existing firm-only stamp from the BOV
                                 chain, untouched.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 DOWNGRADED 2026-08-20. THE SYNC GUARANTEE WAS OVERSTATED. RETRACTED IN PLACE.
        =====================================================================================

            RETRACTED, from the Selected_Broker__c line above: "so the two can never disagree."

        WHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE, AND IT IS NARROWER: the two cannot disagree ACROSS A SAVE OF THE
        DISPOSITION THAT CHANGES Broker__c. That is the only event the stamp responds to.
        DispositionBrokerStampService's pass 1 is literally

            if (d.Broker__c == priorBroker) { continue; }

        so a save that leaves the lookup Id alone never reaches the stamp at all.

        🔴 THE VECTOR THAT WILL ACTUALLY HAPPEN IS A CONTACT RENAME, AND IT IS NOT DEFENDED.
        Selected_Broker__c is composed from the Contact's OWN fields - Name, and Broker_Firm__c
        when present. Editing either of those on the Contact does not touch
        Disposition__c.Broker__c, does not fire DispositionTrigger, and therefore never
        re-stamps. Concretely: a broker moves from CBRE to JLL, or marries and changes surname,
        or the firm is corrected from a typo. Every disposition already pointing at that Contact
        keeps displaying the OLD string - on the record page, in the Broker Selection Path step,
        on the Broker_Selection_Approval page and in every report - PERMANENTLY, because for a
        settled disposition Broker__c never changes again. The lookup is right and the text is
        wrong, silently, forever.

        THIS IS NOT A DEFECT TO FIX HERE, AND DELIBERATELY SO. The obvious "fix" - a Contact
        trigger fanning out to every Brokered_Disposition - is a cross-object write on a
        high-volume standard object to keep a DISPLAY string current, and the display already
        has a correct source: Broker__c renders the Contact as a live, clickable name. WHAT IS
        REQUIRED IS THAT NOBODY TREAT THE TEXT AS AUTHORITATIVE. It is a convenience copy with a
        known staleness window of "until the lookup next changes", which is the honest way to
        describe it and the reason the NO CONSUMER MAY PARSE rule below is not merely stylistic.
        ⚠ THE APPROVER IS THE ONE EXPOSED. Broker_Selection_Approval shows BOTH fields side by
        side precisely so the approver sees the firm without navigating away - so a stale firm
        string is read as current by the one person whose judgement depends on it. The
        mitigation is the clickable lookup next to it, not the text.
        ⚠ ONE MORE ALREADY-DOCUMENTED DISAGREEMENT, listed here so the set is complete: a
        SetNull cascade blanks Broker__c without firing the trigger and leaves the text behind.
        That one has a guard - see the SEED HAZARD block below.

        ⚠ ONE FIELD, TWO FORMATS. Selected_Broker__c reads 'Firm' on on-market rows and
        'Person - Firm' on off-market rows. Deliberate: on-market approves a firm's BOV response,
        off-market approves a named individual. NO CONSUMER MAY PARSE THIS FIELD. Nothing parses
        it today; keep it that way.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 SEED HAZARD. scripts/seed-broker-contacts.apex CAN SILENTLY NULL THIS FIELD.
        =====================================================================================
        That script opens at line 4 with:

            delete [SELECT Id FROM Contact WHERE Is_Broker__c = true];

        With deleteConstraint SetNull, re-running that seed NULLS Broker__c on every Disposition
        holding it, with no error and no warning. The damage is invisible, and that is what makes
        it severe: the denormalised Selected_Broker__c text is left behind still naming the
        vanished broker, so the record looks healthy on the page, in the Path step and on any
        report, while the approval gate now refuses the submit. The same exposure already applies
        to Broker_Assignment__c.Broker__c, Lease_Inquiry__c.Broker__c and Opportunity.Broker__c,
        which point at the same Contacts with the same semantics.

        MITIGATION, AS SPECIFIED IN THE REQUIREMENTS (offmarket-broker-lookup-requirements.md,
        section 2.6 and risk R1). All three parts, not one of them:
          1. A GUARD IN THE SCRIPT. seed-broker-contacts.apex must refuse to run when any
             Disposition references a broker Contact, e.g. abort with a clear message when
             [SELECT COUNT() FROM Disposition__c WHERE Broker__c != null] > 0, unless an
             explicit FORCE = true flag is set in the script.
          2. A LOUD HEADER COMMENT on that script naming all four dependent lookups as
             collateral of its line 4.
          3. TREAT THE SCRIPT AS REBUILD ONLY. Never run it against an org holding real
             disposition data.
        🔴 DO NOT "FIX" THIS BY SWITCHING TO deleteConstraint Restrict. That would make the seed
        fail loudly but would also block legitimate Contact deletion for the whole org.

        ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-08-20 (re-review). This block read:

            RETRACTED: "That script opens at line 4 with:
                delete [SELECT Id FROM Contact WHERE Is_Broker__c = true];
            ...The guard in part 1 is NOT in this deliverable. It is a separate edit to
            scripts/seed-broker-contacts.apex and it is still OPEN as of this file's creation."

        Both halves are now false. (1) The inline delete no longer exists: it was hoisted into
        a six-step structure (STEP 1 scan basis, STEP 2 guard scan, STEP 3 abort gate, STEP 4
        force-path clear, STEP 5 explicit Database.delete, STEP 6 reseed) with a working
        pre-DML guard and an abort gate, exactly as part 1 above specifies. (2) The predicate
        is now the Contact.Broker record type, not Is_Broker__c - which MADE THE HAZARD REAL
        rather than reducing it: under the old predicate the delete matched zero rows org-wide;
        under the new one it matches the org's ~19 live brokers. See the reconciled header of
        scripts/seed-broker-contacts.apex for the full write-up, including why the guard binds
        to the doomed Contact set rather than to `Broker__c != null`.

        =====================================================================================
        RELATIONSHIP NAME: Brokered_Dispositions. CHOSEN FOR MEANING, NOT FORCED BY A COLLISION.
        =====================================================================================
        COLLISION CHECK FIRST, MEASURED NOT ASSUMED: exactly three fields in this repo carry
        referenceTo Contact, and they use Broker_Assignments, Lease_Inquiries and
        Brokered_Opportunities. Nothing on Contact uses Dispositions OR Brokered_Dispositions,
        so BOTH names were available. The Dispositions that does exist in this repo belongs to
        Disposition__c.Property_Asset__c, whose parent is Property_Asset__c; relationship names
        are unique PER PARENT OBJECT, so that one never contended for the Contact namespace.

        WHY Brokered_Dispositions AND NOT THE BARE PLURAL. Under Contact, "Dispositions" reads as
        "this Contact's dispositions" when what the related list actually means is "dispositions
        this Contact BROKERS". Opportunity.Broker__c is the org's own precedent for exactly this
        shape, a ROLE named Contact lookup on a deal object, and it is called
        Brokered_Opportunities. Broker_Assignments and Lease_Inquiries take the bare plural
        because those objects exist ONLY to be about a broker, so the role is already in the
        object name; Disposition__c and Opportunity are not broker objects, so the role has to be
        carried by the relationship name.
        ⚠ A relationshipName is disruptive to change after deploy. It was settled here, before
        the first deploy, while it cost nothing.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 FLS IS LOAD BEARING FOR THIS FIELD, AND THE BLAST RADIUS IS THE WHOLE OBJECT.
        =====================================================================================
        A Metadata API deployed custom field arrives with NO FLS for any profile, System
        Administrator included. This field joins the SELECT in
        DispositionSelector.selectApprovalContextById, which is WITH USER_MODE and backs ALL FOUR
        approval branches. USER_MODE THROWS, IT DOES NOT DEGRADE: one unreadable field raises
        System.QueryException "No such column", an FLS denial wearing a schema error, and Submit
        for Approval breaks on EVERY disposition at EVERY stage on BOTH record types, not just
        the off-market broker step.

        Read is granted in DPEG_Disposition_View and edit in DPEG_Disposition_Edit in the same
        deploy as this field. Those two files and this one must land TOGETHER, and never Apex
        first. 🔴 WHEN THAT QueryException APPEARS, THE FIX IS THE PERMISSION SET, NOT
        WITH SYSTEM_MODE. SYSTEM_MODE is banned for that method by its own class header
        (DispositionSelector.cls:444-456), which argues USER_MODE is the right answer there
        rather than the default. DPEG_Admin_Access is deliberately NOT widened: it grants only
        four Disposition__c fields today and a bare administrator holding no disposition persona
        set already cannot run that query, so this change creates no new admin regression.

        ⚠ VISIBILITY IS SCOPED IN THE FLEXIPAGE, NOT HERE. The field renders only where
        Is_On_Market__c is false, via a fieldInstance level visibilityRule on
        Disposition_Record_Page. There is no record-type guard on the field itself and none
        should be added; the stamp service keys on Broker__c changing, so the scoping is
        structural rather than duplicated.

        ⚠ TESTS MUST USE TestDataFactory.createBrokerContact / createBrokerContacts. The lookup
        filter below is ACTIVE and NOT optional, so a plain createContacts() Contact will be
        refused.
    -->
    <fullName>Broker__c</fullName>
    <deleteConstraint>SetNull</deleteConstraint>
    <description>OFF-MARKET ONLY. The broker Contact directly picked for this disposition, and the field the Broker Selection approval gate tests. Selected_Broker__c is stamped from this lookup as a display string and is retained because the on-market BOV chain still writes text into it. See the XML comment in this file.</description>
    <inlineHelpText>Pick the broker for this off-market deal. Only Contacts on the Broker record type appear. Selected Broker fills in automatically from your pick, so do not type into it.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>Broker</label>
    <lookupFilter>
        <active>true</active>
        <booleanFilter>1</booleanFilter>
        <errorMessage>Only Broker Contacts can be selected here.</errorMessage>
        <filterItems>
            <field>Contact.RecordType.DeveloperName</field>
            <operation>equals</operation>
            <value>Broker</value>
        </filterItems>
        <isOptional>false</isOptional>
    </lookupFilter>
    <referenceTo>Contact</referenceTo>
    <relationshipLabel>Brokered Dispositions</relationshipLabel>
    <relationshipName>Brokered_Dispositions</relationshipName>
    <required>false</required>
    <trackHistory>false</trackHistory>
    <trackTrending>false</trackTrending>
    <type>Lookup</type>
</CustomField>
